/ref 17592186069619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069618 #edb/ref 1061 165691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069618 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e261bc60-5f18-5ce3-8444-d66fb71d6b56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069618 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069619 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069619 #edb/ref 1061 165695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069619 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f81f6359-676f-52fc-9e50-90a17ad8c71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069619 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069620 #edb/ref 1061 165699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "116d4e70-b784-54f1-b8a9-1880b475e2a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1064 "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1065 8692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ef92091-e041-5715-ac4e-75fa146c4cea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 